With only GEMINI_API_KEY set, yoyo now starts on google. Before, it stopped with "No API key found". I re-ran the exact repro on the built binary with a dummy key, and the request now reaches Google, which rejects it with 400 "API key not valid". That means the key was found and sent. With no key at all, the "No API key found" error still fires. Build, the full test suite, clippy and fmt all pass. The commit is 121fed09 and it's pushed.

What changed
• `src/providers.rs`: a new provider_api_key_vars(provider) lists every variable yoagent reads a key from for that provider, in yoagent's order (google: GEMINI_API_KEY, then GOOGLE_API_KEY). It also keeps two yoyo-only entries, each with a comment saying why: github uses GITHUB_TOKEN, and bedrock uses AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID. Bedrock leaves out yoagent's bearer-token variable, because apply_bedrock_credentials would wrongly join it with the secret. A small function, find_provider_key, does the lookup and takes the environment as a closure, so the tests never touch the real environment. provider_api_key_env still returns a single name, so hint text doesn't change.
• `src/cli.rs`: the key lookup is now one call to that function. The ANTHROPIC_API_KEY → API_KEY fallback is untouched.
• `src/dispatch_sub.rs`: this file wasn't in the plan. Its resolve_api_key had the same one-variable lookup, so I gave it the same one-call swap. Leaving it would have kept the bug in a second place.

Tests (in a new src/providers_key_tests.rs)
• Table of cases: GEMINI only, GOOGLE only, an empty GEMINI with GOOGLE set, anthropic, openai falling back to API_KEY, github, bedrock, nothing set, and GEMINI not leaking into openai.
• Check against yoagent's own lookup: the brief asked me to use yoagent's api_key_variables, but in yoagent 0.25.12 that function takes no provider, and the per-provider list is private. So I copied the list into yoyo, and this test checks the copy against yoagent's real resolve_api_key. It re-runs its own test binary in a child process that sets each variable alone, then compares both the variable names and which one wins, for every known provider. A child process is the only place env vars can be changed without racing other tests.
• Proof the tests catch the bug: I broke the code twice on purpose, one at a time, and put it back each time. Dropping GEMINI_API_KEY from google made the table and the yoagent check both fail with messages naming it. Swapping google's order made it fail with "yoyo's precedence differs from yoagent's".

Docs: the google rows in models.md, installation.md and fork.md now say "GEMINI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_API_KEY". I also added a short note under providers.rs in ARCHITECTURE.md.

Not done in this slice
I posted the list of what's left on #1064 with file:line for each. Several other places still look the key up through the single-name function, so for google they only see GOOGLE_API_KEY:
• the sub-agent fallback key (tools.rs:1519)
• the model fallback key (agent_builder.rs:1951)
• /spawn
• /doctor
• the setup wizard's needs_setup, which can still open for a user who only has GEMINI_API_KEY

The hint messages also still name one variable. Bedrock's bearer token and yoagent's generic YOAGENT_API_KEY aren't read. Point 2 of #1064 (scrubbing keys from MCP servers and hooks by default) is untouched.
